# Write tests for code

Any change to behaviour comes with tests in the same change. This applies to
features, bug fixes, refactors and generated code alike. "It typechecks" and "I
tried it" are not tests.

*   **New behaviour:** a test that fails without the change. A bug fix starts with a
    test that reproduces the bug.
*   **Refactors:** keep the existing tests green, and add tests for any behaviour they
    didn't cover that you touched or are now relying on. If you can't show the old
    behaviour is preserved, the refactor isn't done.
*   **Cover the unhappy paths** that matter here: validation failures, 404s, refusals
    (published items, wrong parent, non-editors), and integrity rules (cascades,
    citations, duplicate links).
*   **Test at the layer that owns the rule.** Integrity lives in `ContentService`, so test it
    there (`content.service.spec.ts`); HTTP wiring, guards and validation in `app.spec.ts`;
    the connector end to end in `mcp.e2e.spec.ts`; pure model logic in `packages/domain`.
    Add an HTTP or MCP test only for what that layer adds.
*   **Use the existing tools:** vitest, `@nestjs/testing`, and `InMemoryContentStore`. No
    network, no AWS, no real secrets; tests must pass offline.
*   **Coverage is enforced.** `pnpm test` in `apps/bff` and `packages/domain` runs with coverage and
    fails below 90% on statements, branches, functions and lines (`vitest.shared.ts`); CI runs it, so an
    untested change fails the PR. Check where you stand with `pnpm --filter @greed/bff test`. Never lower
    the threshold or widen an exclude to get green: write the test. The only excludes are process entry
    points (`main.ts`, `lambda.ts`, `local-tables.ts`) and re-export barrels. Name any new one in the config
    and explain why. `data/seed` and `infra/cdk` are not yet gated, nor is `apps/web` (it has no tests yet).
*   **Docs, types and config-only changes** don't need new tests, but say so in the summary.
*   **If you can't test it,** say what is untested and why. Don't leave it out silently.

Before calling work done, run the full check from `greed-architecture` ("Verify") and report the
result honestly: failures included, skipped steps named.
